<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emerald Buddha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Phra Kaeo Morakot</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Emerald Buddha is a small statue of the seated Buddha carved from green jade, the palladium of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. Kept for centuries in Lan Na, it was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> and then Vientiane in the 16th century. Chao Phraya Chakri carried it off to Thonburi in 1779, and since 1784 it has stood in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phra-kaew">Wat Phra Kaew</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha.jpg" alt="Emerald Buddha" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: กสิณธร ราชโอรส · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%A8%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B5%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%A8%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%AA%E0%B8%94%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A1%E0%B9%81%E0%B8%A5%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%9A%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%8A%E0%B8%A7%E0%B8%B1%E0%B8%87_%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%82%E0%B8%95%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B0%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%A3_%E0%B8%81%E0%B8%A3%E0%B8%B8%E0%B8%87%E0%B9%80%E0%B8%97%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%A1%E0%B8%AB%E0%B8%B2%E0%B8%99%E0%B8%84%E0%B8%A3_(51).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The legend of the image is told in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinakalamali">Jinakalamali</a>, the Amarakatabuddharupanidana and above all the Ratanabimbavamsa, a Pali chronicle by Brahmarājaprajña of the 15th century, whose oldest surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> dates only to 1788. According to the legend the sage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasena">Nagasena</a> made the image in 43 BCE at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>, with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a>. Historical sources instead place its appearance in the Lan Na kingdom of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> in 1434, when lightning is said to have struck a chedi at Wat Pa Yia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-rai">Chiang Rai</a> and revealed a stucco-covered Buddha. The abbot saw green stone where the stucco on the nose had flaked off.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Emerald Buddha is an image of the Buddha seated in meditation, about 66 centimetres high and 48 centimetres wide at the lap. It is made of a semi-precious green stone, described as jade or jasper, since emerald refers in Thai to its colour. The image has not been analysed to establish its composition or origin. Its posture suggests the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-saen">Chiang Saen</a> or Chiang Mai school, not much earlier than the 15th century, although its meditation attitude resembles Buddha images of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, which has led some to suggest an origin there. It is clothed in gold and regarded as the sacred palladium of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The legend says King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sam-fang-kaen">Sam Fang Kaen</a> of Lan Na wanted the image for Chiang Mai, but the elephant carrying it insisted three times on going to Lampang, where it stayed 32 years. In 1468 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tilokarat">Tilokaraj</a> moved it to Chiang Mai. In 1552 it was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setthathirath">Setthathirath</a> of Lan Xang adopted it as his personal palladium; in 1564 he moved it to Vientiane, where it stayed 214 years. In 1779 the Siamese general Chao Phraya Chakri looted Vientiane and took it to Thonburi. He later founded the Chakri Dynasty as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phra-kaew">Wat Phra Kaew</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>'s Grand Palace.</p><p>The image wears three sets of gold seasonal decoration, two made by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> and one by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a>. The king or a senior royal changes them at the start of each season. Early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> period the image was paraded to relieve plague and cholera, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongkut">Rama IV</a> ended the practice, believing that diseases are caused by germs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emerald Buddha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Emerald Buddha</b>, now Thailand's most sacred image, was kept in Chiang Mai from 1468 until the early 1550s, when it was taken to Lan Xang.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal legitimacy rested on descent from the legendary ancestor Khun Borom, on patronage of Buddhism and on possession of sacred images such as the Phra Bang and, from Setthathirath's reign, the <b>Emerald Buddha</b>, which he brought from Lan Na after briefly ruling there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Grand Palace and Wat Phra Kaew rose at its centre, and in 1784 the <b>Emerald Buddha</b>, which Chakri himself had brought from Vientiane in 1779, was installed there as the protective image of the realm.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Vientiane kept the old capital and much of the Lan Xang heritage, including the <b>Emerald Buddha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-vientiane">Kingdom of Vientiane</a></li><li><blockquote>His generals, the brothers Chao Phraya Chakri and Surasi, expelled the Burmese from Lan Na and conquered the Kingdom of Vientiane in 1779, bringing back the <b>Emerald Buddha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/thonburi-kingdom">Thonburi Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
